# The full gate, and the only one: build, lint, both spec lints, format check, the contract-bundle
# and cross-repo contract checks, then unit, cross-cutting, integration and contract tests.
#
# About 90 seconds, which is why it is not split into tiers. Integration and contract cost 14s and
# 49s against an in-memory Mongo — there is nothing worth trading away, and one gate is easier to
# trust than three.
#
# `check:spec-identity` SKIPS rather than fails when the paired frontend is not on disk, so a
# half-cloned pair can still commit. It stays fatal under CI, where a missing sibling means the
# workflow is misconfigured.
#
# `test:fuzz` IS here, inside `npm run test`. It was held back to nightly while the 500 it found on
# `POST /products/search` was open; that is fixed, and the suite has since caught two more of the
# same shape — a bad request answered as a server fault. It earns its place in the gate.
#
# Deliberately not here, and run by hand for the occasions that want them: `test:mutation`
# (re-executes the unit suite once per mutant — nightly in CI), `complete:manual` (`test:prism`,
# which binds a real port), and `bench` / `bench:search` (need a running server, and report numbers
# rather than a verdict).
# Write the generated artefacts first, then verify them. Regenerating here rather than leaving it
# to whoever remembers is what stops a commit failing the gate with "STALE" for a file no human
# authors. Costs about five seconds: contracts:bundle 1s, gen:api 1s, gen:asyncapi 2s,
# seed:export 1s.
#
# `--no-sync` is not optional. A full `regenerate` ends with `sync:frontend`, which writes into the
# PAIRED REPO's working tree — a commit here would silently dirty a checkout you are not looking
# at, and possibly one with its own work in progress. Hand the files over deliberately, with
# `npm run regenerate` by itself, when you mean to.
#
# `git add` is required: a file rewritten after staging is not in the commit, so the gate below
# would pass against content that is not what gets committed. Note this widens a partial
# `git add -p` to include the whole regenerated output — which is what you want for a generated
# file, and a surprise exactly once.
#
# `docs/modules/` and `docs/api/endpoints.md` are on the list for the same reason `api/` is:
# `regenerate` ends with `docs:modules`, which rewrites the generated blocks of every module page
# and the app-wide endpoint table from the manifests, routers and schemas. Leave them off and
# `check:module-docs` verifies a working tree that is fresher than the commit — green gate, stale
# pages.
npm run regenerate -- --no-sync
git add openapi.yaml asyncapi.yaml asyncapi.public.yaml api src/types/asyncapi.generated.ts \
        db/demo/demo-data.json src/infrastructure/observability/analytics-events.frontend.ts \
        docs/modules docs/api/endpoints.md

npm run complete
